WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:02.980
ED GLAESER: If you live in a city, then you trade in that city.

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:06.730
You buy, perhaps breakfast or a newspaper or something more exciting.

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:09.857
You sell as well, probably your time to an employer.

00:00:09.857 --> 00:00:12.850
You may well have come to your city precisely because you could sell

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:15.100
your time at a higher price.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.730
In this section, we are going to explore how cities make markets, and

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:20.050
how makes make cities.

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:23.390
And hopefully, you will come to understand your city a little bit better.

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:26.466
Cities typically have a wider range of services.

00:00:26.466 --> 00:00:27.980
Scale makes that possible.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:31.020
Meaning that there are more types of restaurants, more rare goods, and

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:35.800
more specialized services like nail salons or bespoke tailors or fortune tellers.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:39.340
Internet retailing has leveled the playing field between city and town for

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:43.160
many products, but not for services that require face to face contact.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:44.470
Not for restaurants, and not for

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:48.420
higher end products that you want to see before you buy, like diamonds.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:51.340
Cities enable labor markets to work more efficiently.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:54.940
You can hop from job to job more easily in a big metropolitan area.

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:57.480
You can weather a mass lay off more readily in a city.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:01.510
And that helps explain why joblessness is lower in metropolitan America.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:02.580
If you run a business,

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:06.340
you will find it easier to find the labor you need in a large metropolitan area.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:09.770
The success of cities often depends on how they do as markets.

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:13.020
When labor markets break down, as they did in Detroit after the faltering

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.830
of the Big Three automakers, the city itself spiraled into decline.

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:19.400
The collapse of retail on main streets in the US and

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:23.505
high streets in the UK has often left a great emptiness in the heart of the city.

00:01:23.505 --> 00:01:27.190
Internet-based markets like Uber and Lyft enter into urban areas and

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:29.360
they take advantage of urban scale.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.870
Yet they also upset regulators

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:35.200
who often try to protect incumbent businesses from competition.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.320
Understanding the interplay between cities and

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:41.560
trade is helpful if you want to understand how to get the most out of your city or

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:44.520
if you wanna make a guess about what's its future will be.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.940
Or if you just want to sensibly discuss policies that impact trade,

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:50.560
like the regulation of Uber or food trucks.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.930
Hopefully, these videos will help you to better understand the past and

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:55.530
future of commercial cities.

